l examples, helping to solidify the modulo pattern I started to formulate. This pattern is pivotal.\n\n\n\n**Refining Diagonal Logic**\n\nI'm now revisiting the diagonal pattern, this time with a more rigorous approach. I'm focusing on why '1' appears only from row 3 onwards and how it relates to the modulo pattern. The spacing of the '2's at each row is key, because the valid '1' placement falls within the bounds of those '2's, based on `col % 4`. I'm using examples with varying 'c' values to derive a generalized rule.\n\n\n\n**Defining Modulo Equation**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I realized the placement of '1's isn't based on an offset. It's a fundamental diagonal strip with a period of 4! The condition is now simplified: `(col - row) % 4 == c % 4`. This elegantly describes the placement. I've re-examined various test cases to confirm that it holds across different row positions and matrix sizes.\n\n\n",
